-- title:
--   Commuting Atkin–Lehner involutions from Čerednik descent data
-- statement:
--   The data are the following.
--
--   Arithmetic side. Rationals $a_2,b_2$ and naturals $N,q,q'$ with $N$ nonzero and squarefree, $q$ and $q'$ prime, $q\nmid N$, $q'\nmid N$, $q'\neq q$, $5\le q$ and $5\le q'$. The hypothesis `hdef₂` says that $a_2<0$, $b_2<0$ and that for every height-one prime $v$ of $\mathcal O_{\mathbb Q}$ the completion $\mathbb H[\mathbb Q,a_2,b_2]\otimes_{\mathbb Q}\mathbb Q_v$ is a division ring exactly when $q\in v$; thus $B=\mathbb H[\mathbb Q,a_2,b_2]$ is definite and ramified precisely at $q$. Two $\mathbb Z$-submodules $\Lambda_2,R_2$ of $B$ are given, with $\Lambda_2$ a maximal order (an order maximal among the orders containing it), $R_2$ an Eichler order of level $N$ (an intersection of two maximal orders, of relative index $N$ in the first) and $R_2\le\Lambda_2$. A unit $n_2$ of $B\otimes_{\mathbb Q}\mathbb A_{\mathbb Q}^{\mathrm{fin}}$ lies in `primeHeckeSet R₂ q'`, i.e. $n_2$ lies in the adelic box of $R_2$ (the additive group generated by the $z\otimes a$ with $z\in R_2$ and $a$ integral at every place), $q'n_2^{-1}$ lies in that box while $n_2^{-1}$ and $q'^{-1}n_2$ do not. Further: `hS₂` says that $\mathrm{meetOrder}(R_2,n_2)=R_2\cap n_2R_2n_2^{-1}$ is an Eichler order of level $Nq'$; `hnorm₂` says that $n_2$ normalises this smaller order; `hsq₂` says that the shift $x\mapsto \mathrm{mk}(\bar x\,n_2)$ on the class set of the finite-idelic stabiliser of $\mathrm{meetOrder}(R_2,n_2)$ (a double coset quotient by the diagonal image of $B^\times$ on one side and the stabiliser on the other) is an involution; the two class sets concerned are finite. The hypothesis `hlaws₂`, the predicate `ClassSetHeckeLaws N q' Λ₂ R₂ n₂`, has four clauses: the edge Hecke matrices pairwise commute, the vertex Hecke matrices pairwise commute, for every prime $\ell\neq q'$ and each of the two degeneracy pushforwards the edge Hecke matrix is carried to the vertex Hecke matrix, and the simultaneous kernel of the two pushforwards is stable under every edge Hecke matrix.
--
--   Geometric side. A valuation subring $A_1$ of $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ with $q'$ in its nonunits, on whose decomposition subgroup over $\mathbb Q$ the valuation is invariant; a height-one prime $v_1$ of $\mathcal O_{\mathbb Q}$ containing $q'$. A field $F_N$ that is a curve over $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ (principal divisors, finite residue fields, $\Omega_{F_N/\overline{\mathbb Q}}$ free of rank one) and essentially of finite type, and a tower $\mathbb T$ of type `HeckeTower.TowerData q q' FN`: for each prime $\ell$ different from $q$ and $q'$ a field $\mathbb T.F\,\ell$ which is again a curve over $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ and essentially of finite type, together with, for each arrow, an $\overline{\mathbb Q}$-algebra map from $F_N$ which is finite and integral. The hypothesis `hfg` requires that each level field $\mathbb T.\mathrm{objField}\,j$ (namely $F_N$ for $j=\mathrm{none}$ and $\mathbb T.F\,\ell$ for $j=\mathrm{some}\,\ell$) contains an element transcendental over $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ over which it is finite-dimensional. Semilinear automorphism groups are used throughout: `SemilinearAut K F` consists of the pairs $(\sigma,\sigma_0)$ of ring automorphisms of $F$ and of $K$ with $\sigma\circ\mathrm{algebraMap}=\mathrm{algebraMap}\circ\sigma_0$, and `baseAut` picks out $\sigma_0$. Given are homomorphisms $\mathrm{gal}_N$ from the decomposition subgroup of $A_1$ over $\mathbb Q$ into $\mathrm{SemilinearAut}(\overline{\mathbb Q},F_N)$ and $\mathrm{gal}_T(\ell)$ into $\mathrm{SemilinearAut}(\overline{\mathbb Q},\mathbb T.F\,\ell)$, whose base components are the given Galois elements themselves (`hgalN`, `hgalT`), and candidate Atkin–Lehner operators $W:\mathrm{Fin}\,2\to\mathrm{SemilinearAut}(\overline{\mathbb Q},F_N)$ and $W^T(\ell):\mathrm{Fin}\,2\to\mathrm{SemilinearAut}(\overline{\mathbb Q},\mathbb T.F\,\ell)$, a priori subject to no condition.
--
--   Mumford side. An injective $\mathbb Q$-algebra map $\iota_1$ of $B$ into $2\times 2$ matrices over the rational closure of $A_1$ (the topological closure of the prime subfield inside the completion of $A_1$'s valuation), the associated projective representation $\rho_1:B^\times\to\mathrm{PGL}_2$ of that closure, given on each unit by the class of $\iota_1$ of it (`hρ₁`), a pseudo-uniformiser $\varpi_1$ of the rational closure in the completion whose image is $q'$ (`hϖ₁`), and the assumption that the ring `HolRingOf ϖ₁ ρ₁` of functions on the upper half-plane holomorphic on each affinoid is a domain.
--
--   Level data. Families $s_1$ of units of $B$ and $s^{\mathrm f}_1$ of finite idelic units indexed by the primes $\ell\notin\{q,q'\}$; the hypothesis `hs₁` requires, for each such $\ell$: the component of $s^{\mathrm f}_1(\ell)$ at every place $u$ not containing $q'$ is $s_1(\ell)\otimes 1$; its component at every place containing $q'$ is $1$; the product of the diagonal finite idele attached to the scalar $\ell$ with $s^{\mathrm f}_1(\ell)^{-1}$ lies in `levelHeckeUSet Λ₂ (meetOrder R₂ n₂) ℓ` if $\ell\mid N$ and in `primeHeckeSet (meetOrder R₂ n₂) ℓ` otherwise; and $\mathrm{nrd}(s_1(\ell))=\ell$. Level groups $\Gamma_1(j)\le B^\times$ are prescribed by `hΓ₁0` at the base, $\Gamma_1(\mathrm{none})$ consisting of the units lying in `awayUnits R₂ v₁` (that is, whose image at every finite place $u\neq v_1$ lies in the subgroup generated by the local unit box of $R_2$ at $u$) and with $\mathrm{ord}_{q'}\mathrm{nrd}$ even, and by `hΓ₁ℓ` at the raised levels, $\Gamma_1(\mathrm{some}\,\ell)=\Gamma_1(\mathrm{none})\cap s_1(\ell)\Gamma_1(\mathrm{none})s_1(\ell)^{-1}$. Atkin–Lehner elements $w_1,\bar w_1$ indexed by the levels satisfy: `hw₁`, that $w_1(\mathrm{none})\in$ `awayUnits R₂ v₁` with $\mathrm{nrd}=q'$, and $w_1(\mathrm{some}\,\ell)\in$ `awayUnits (meetOrder R₂ (sf₁ ℓ)) v₁` with $\mathrm{nrd}=q'$; and `hwbar₁`, that $\mathrm{nrd}(\bar w_1(\mathrm{none}))=q$, that at every place $u\neq v_1$ not containing $q$ the image of $\bar w_1(\mathrm{none})$ lies in the local unit box of $R_2$, and that at every place $u\neq v_1$ conjugation $x\mapsto \bar w_1(\mathrm{none})^{-1}x\,\bar w_1(\mathrm{none})$ preserves both the local box of $R_2$ and the local box of $\Lambda_2$ (each as an equivalence), with the same three conditions at every raised level $\ell$, with $\mathrm{meetOrder}(R_2,s^{\mathrm f}_1(\ell))$ in place of $R_2$.
--
--   Descent data. A homomorphism $d_{\mathrm{Iso}_1}$ from the decomposition subgroup into the isometric automorphisms of the completion over the rational closure, realising on the completion the Galois action (`hdIso₁`); a character $\chi_1$ of the decomposition subgroup with values in $\mathrm{Multiplicative}(\mathbb Z/2)$; ring maps $\iota_{M_1}(j)$ from each level field into the fraction field of `HolRingOf ϖ₁ ρ₁`; and finally the hypothesis `hI`, which asserts the predicate `DescentIntertwining` with $r=q'$ and index pair $(1,0)$ for the data $A_1,\rho_1,\varpi_1,\Gamma_1,w_1,\bar w_1,s_1,d_{\mathrm{Iso}_1},F_N,\mathbb T,\mathrm{gal}_N,\mathrm{gal}_T,W,W^T,\chi_1,\iota_{M_1}$. Its first clauses require that $\chi_1$ be trivial on the inertia subgroup, nontrivial at every Frobenius element at $q'$, and trivial exactly on those $\tau$ fixing all elements $x$ of the residue field of $A_1$ with $x^{q'^2}=x$, and that each $\iota_{M_1}(j)$ send a constant $z\in\overline{\mathbb Q}$ to its image in the completion; the remaining clauses tie the semilinear actions $\mathrm{gal}_N$, $\mathrm{gal}_T$, $W$, $W^T$ and the level structures $\Gamma_1,w_1,\bar w_1,s_1,d_{\mathrm{Iso}_1}$ to one another through the embeddings $\iota_{M_1}$.
--
--   Under these hypotheses the conclusion is the conjunction of eight assertions, products being taken in the relevant semilinear automorphism group: for each $i\in\mathrm{Fin}\,2$ one has $W_i\,W_i=1$; $W_1W_0=W_0W_1$; for every $\tau$ in the decomposition subgroup of $A_1$ over $\mathbb Q$ and every $i$, $\mathrm{gal}_N(\tau)\,W_i=W_i\,\mathrm{gal}_N(\tau)$; for every $i$ and every $a\in\overline{\mathbb Q}$, $\mathrm{baseAut}(W_i)(a)=a$; for every prime $\ell\notin\{q,q'\}$ and every $i$, $W^T(\ell)_i\,W^T(\ell)_i=1$; for every such $\ell$, $W^T(\ell)_1W^T(\ell)_0=W^T(\ell)_0W^T(\ell)_1$; for every such $\ell$, every $\tau$ and every $i$, $\mathrm{gal}_T(\ell)(\tau)\,W^T(\ell)_i=W^T(\ell)_i\,\mathrm{gal}_T(\ell)(\tau)$; and for every such $\ell$, every $i$ and every $a\in\overline{\mathbb Q}$, $\mathrm{baseAut}(W^T(\ell)_i)(a)=a$.
--
--   This is the Atkin–Lehner package on the Čerednik–Drinfeld side at a place above $q'$: the two candidate operators at each level of the Hecke tower are involutions, they commute with each other and with the semilinear action of the decomposition group, and they act trivially on the field of constants $\overline{\mathbb Q}$. It is the input used by [`CerednikDrinfeld.exists_symmetryGroup_semilinearAction_invariantFieldOf_of_descentIntertwining_one_zero`](thm.html#CerednikDrinfeld.exists_symmetryGroup_semilinearAction_invariantFieldOf_of_descentIntertwining_one_zero), where these relations are what make the operators generate a finite symmetry group acting semilinearly on the tower.
